            <ArticleTitle>Unmasking of J waves during right coronary angiography in patients with spontaneous coronary spasms and ventricular fibrillation.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>185-8</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>We encountered two consecutive cases with spontaneous ST elevation due to right coronary spasms and subsequent ventricular fibrillation (VF). Their 12-lead ECGs on anterior chest pain showed elevation of ST-segments in the inferior leads, but coronary angiography (CAG) revealed no significant stenosis. Both cases showed dramatically evolving J waves in the inferior leads during the right CAG, but it was not observed during angiography of the left CAG. Neither Brugada-type ECG nor long-QT was evident. In summary, J waves can be produced without ST-segment elevation, and contrast media-induced J waves might be related to the arrhythmogenesis of subsequent VF evoked by right coronary spasms.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
